    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: Riders With A Passenger Pre-Ride Inspection</h1>
    <p>A pre-ride inspection is a quiet five minutes that decides how the rest of the day behaves, and with a passenger aboard it stops being optional courtesy and becomes the rider's honest obligation. Every fault the inspection looks for, soft tires, weak brakes, loose controls, glazed cables, matters twice on a two-up machine, because the machine carries twice the people and the rider answers for both. The inspection is the same checklist the solo world uses, read with a passenger's stakes.</p>,    <p>This page is the inspection chapter of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster for riders with a passenger. The verified fleet and its prices are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, general questions are on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the machine-matching decision that comes before this one is the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-bike-choice/">bike choice page for riders with a passenger</a>.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Why the passenger doubles the stakes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and sidewalls</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Brakes: the front-rear test</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Lights, horn and signals</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">The condition record and the phone</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">The five-minute two-up routine</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the passenger doubles the stakes</h2>
    <p>The physics is plain: the same tire that grips adequately for one rider works harder for two, the same brake line that stops a solo machine stops a machine carrying a second person's momentum, and the same wobble that a solo rider corrects with a footplant becomes a two-person event at a stop light. The inspection is where the rider audits those margins before the road does, and it is also where the rider, standing in for a passenger who cannot see the machine's condition, exercises the responsibility the passenger has delegated.</p>
    <p>The habit this page teaches is older than any rental counter: walk the machine, test what moves, note what is wrong, and only then put the second helmet on. It takes five minutes, it needs no tools, and it works on every machine in the fleet, from the lightest 50cc scooter to the strongest automatic, because the checklist reads functions, not models.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Tires: pressure, tread and sidewalls</h2>
    <p>Tires are the first stop because they carry the pair's entire argument with the road. The two-up check has three parts. Pressure, felt at the sidewall with a hard thumb press on both tires, looking for the soft, yielding feel of an under-inflated tire, which with a passenger aboard turns every pothole into a rim-strike and every wet corner into a slide. Tread, read at the center band where the wear lives, looking for the flattened, shiny strip that says the tire is wearing out of its grip. Sidewalls, scanned for cracks, cuts and bulges, which are the tire's way of announcing retirement.</p>
    <p>Two-up tires deserve a stricter eye than solo tires, and the reason is arithmetic: the rear tire that was fine at one rider's load is now working near its design ceiling, and the honest answer at the counter is to ask for a different machine if anything on the rear tire looks tired. Rental fleets live on their tires, and a shop that maintains them will never resent the question, which is one of the quiet indicators of a shop worth renting from twice.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Brakes: the front-rear test</h2>
    <p>The brake test is the inspection's core, and with a passenger it is done in two stages, stopped and rolling. Stopped: pull the front lever and push the bike forward, feeling for a firm, immediate bite, then the rear pedal with the same expectation, and no lever should travel to the bar or floor before engaging. Rolling, at walking pace in a clear space: both brakes, one at a time, checking that each slows the machine confidently and that neither grabs, shudders or squeals its disagreement.</p>
    <p>The two-up reading of the brake test is about margins rather than function. A brake that is adequate solo can be marginal loaded, so the question the rider answers in the rolling test is not does it stop, but does it stop with room to spare, and any brake that needs the full lever before it speaks up is a machine to hand back politely. The inspection continues regardless, because the counter conversation about what was found is part of the process, and the paperwork side of that conversation is on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-rental-paperwork-questions/">paperwork questions page for riders with a passenger</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Controls, cables and the mirrors</h2>
    <p>The controls are tested with the machine standing still and the rider seated in riding posture, because a passenger changes the rider's geometry and the controls must still fall to hand. Throttle: snaps closed from every position, no sticking, no roughness, because a throttle that hangs open for a heartbeat is a machine that accelerates when the pair meant to slow. Clutch or automatic engagement, depending on the fleet tier: smooth take-up, no slip under a firm launch. Cables, where visible: no frayed ends, no cracked housings, no awkward routing.</p>
    <p>Mirrors are a two-up topic more than they look, because the rider with a passenger has a narrower lane of shoulder-check freedom and leans harder on the mirrors. Both mirrors need to be present, intact and actually adjustable to the rider's eye line, and the fifteen seconds it takes to set them at the counter is repaid at every junction for the rest of the rental. The horn, tested once, politely, matters too, because in Hanoi traffic the horn is a language and a mute machine is a silent one.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Lights, horn and signals</h2>
    <p>Lights get their own minute because the pair will, sooner or later, ride at dusk, and dusk in Hanoi arrives with traffic still at full flow. Headlight on and both beams working if the machine has them, brake light reacting to both the lever and the pedal, and turn signals, if the machine has them, flashing at a healthy rhythm, are the three checks. Any one of them dead is a conversation with the counter, and a rider with a passenger owes the conversation, not the workaround.</p>
    <p>The signal check has a passenger-specific addendum: the passenger should know what the machine's signals mean too, because a pair that both read the bike rides better than a pair where only one does. It sounds small, and it is, the way all the good inspection habits are small, five-minute habits that prevent the large, loud kind of surprise. Riders who want the traffic version of the same care can continue to the preparation pages the cluster keeps for loaded and solo audiences, then return to the two-up route and pickup planning when the machine passes.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">The condition record and the phone</h2>
    <p>The inspection ends where it should have begun, with the record. Before the first ride, the rider photographs the machine from four sides, close enough to show existing scratches, dents and worn patches, and photographs the odometer and any marks the shop has written on the rental slip. The photos take ninety seconds, live on the rider's phone, and settle every condition question the return day could raise, which is why the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/riders-with-a-passenger-return-checklist/">return checklist page for riders with a passenger</a> starts with them.</p>
    <p>The record also belongs in the conversation, not just the camera: anything the inspection found, a chipped mirror, a tired tire, a slow signal, gets said at the counter and written or photographed onto the record of the agreement. The shop hears these notes every day, and the pair that hands them over calmly rides away with both the machine and the relationship in better condition.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The five-minute two-up routine</h2>
    <p>Compressed, the routine runs: thumb both tires, push-test the front brake, pedal-test the rear, roll-test both at walking pace, snap the throttle, set the mirrors, try the horn and lights, photograph four sides plus the odometer, and say out loud anything worth saying. Five minutes, no tools, and it works identically on the third day of the rental as on the first, because machines age during rentals and the inspection is a daily habit, not a pickup-day ceremony.</p>
    <p>The rider with a passenger runs this routine for two, and that is the entire difference from the solo version: the same checks, read with doubled stakes, done every riding day. The machine that passes gets the pair; the machine that does not gets a polite counter conversation and, usually, a better machine. Either outcome is a good day's work for five minutes.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Quick questions</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How do I inspect a rental motorbike before riding two-up in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>Run the five-minute routine: check both tires for pressure, tread and sidewall condition, push-test and roll-test both brakes, confirm the throttle snaps closed, set both mirrors, test horn and lights, then photograph all four sides of the machine plus the odometer before the first ride. With a passenger, read every result with doubled stakes and raise anything doubtful at the counter before riding.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Which inspection checks matter most with a passenger aboard?</h2>
      <p>The rear tire and both brakes, because a passenger adds mass exactly where those components carry it. An under-inflated rear tire or a marginal brake that would be acceptable solo becomes a genuine margin problem with two aboard. When in doubt, ask for a different machine rather than negotiate with a tired one.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should I photograph the rental bike even if the shop documented its condition?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Ninety seconds of four-side photos plus the odometer, on your own phone, settles any condition question at return regardless of what the slip says. It protects the pair, it protects the shop, and it takes less time than the helmet strap took to explain.</p>
    </article>

